 "text": "**Monzo is usually the simpler and often cheaper choice for typical UK trips to Europe**, especially on the free plan. Revolut can be better if you travel often, want to hold euros, or use a paid plan.\n\nBoth beat traditional high-street banks (which often add 2–3%+ fees). Europe is mostly in the EEA (EU countries plus Iceland, Liechtenstein and Norway), which matters for Monzo’s cash rules. Switzerland is not in the EEA.\n\n### Monzo (free plan)\n- **Card spending**: Fee-free in any currency. You get the Mastercard rate with no markup and no weekend surcharge, with no monthly cap.\n- **ATM withdrawals in the UK & EEA**:\n  - Unlimited and fee-free if Monzo is your main account (generally: at least £500 paid in over a rolling 35 days plus an active Direct Debit, or certain benefits/student loan payments).\n  - Otherwise £400 fee-free every rolling 30 days, then 3%.\n- **Outside the EEA**: £200 fee-free every 30 days, then 3% (even if it’s your main account).\n- Full UK bank with FSCS protection (up to £120,000). Strong budgeting tools, pots, instant notifications, and an overdraft option if approved.\n- Very straightforward: just spend. No need to pre-exchange or watch weekend rules.\n\n### Revolut (Standard free plan)\n- **Spending/exchange**: Uses Revolut’s rate (near interbank). Free up to a £1,000 monthly exchange allowance, then a 1% fair-usage fee. An extra 1% weekend fee applies on exchanges (roughly Friday evening to Sunday evening, specifically 5pm Friday–6pm Sunday Eastern Time), including automatic conversions when you spend or withdraw without holding the currency.\n- **ATM**: £200 or 5 withdrawals per month (whichever comes first), then 2% (minimum £1). This applies in Europe too.\n- **Big advantage**: Multi-currency balances. You can exchange GBP to EUR on a weekday (within the limit) and hold euros, then spend from the euro balance with no further exchange. SEPA euro transfers are free.\n- Paid plans remove the pain points. Premium (around £8.99/month) gives unlimited exchange with no weekend fee and a higher ATM allowance (£400), plus extras such as some insurance or lounge perks on higher tiers.\n- Revolut Bank UK Ltd is now a full UK bank (since March 2026) with FSCS protection up to £120,000 once your account has been migrated (existing customers are being moved in stages; check the app).\n\n### Which to pick for Europe?\n| Situation | Better choice | Why |\n|-----------|---------------|-----|\n| Occasional holidays, mostly card spending, moderate cash | **Monzo** | No caps or weekend fees on spending; potentially unlimited free EEA ATMs if it’s your main account |\n| Frequent travel or high monthly spend abroad | **Revolut** (ideally paid plan) | Multi-currency holding + better limits once you pay |\n| You want to lock in a rate and hold euros | **Revolut** | Easy in-app exchange and balances |\n| You already use one as your main UK account | Stick with that one (or add the other) | Both work well; simplicity wins for most people |\n\n**Many people use both**: Monzo as the everyday UK account (and reliable travel card) and Revolut as a travel/currency wallet.\n\n### Practical tips\n- Always choose to pay or withdraw in **local currency (euros)**, not pounds. Dynamic currency conversion offered by merchants or ATMs usually has a worse rate.\n- ATM operators can still charge their own fee—neither bank controls that. Prefer fee-free machines where possible.\n- Both cards (Mastercard; Revolut can also be Visa) are widely accepted across Europe. Contactless and app controls (freeze card, notifications) work well abroad.\n- Fees and allowances can change—check the latest details in each app under fees/allowances before you travel.\n\nFor a short or typical European trip, Monzo’s predictability usually makes it the easier winner on the free tier. If you travel a lot or specifically want multi-currency tools and perks, Revolut (especially a paid plan) pulls ahead.",
